  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{\u2033}+5{y}^{\u2032}+6y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n- ${y}^{\u2033}+2{y}^{\u2032}+y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n- ${y}^{\u2033}+4{y}^{\u2032}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping.",
  "worked_solution": "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{\u2033}+5{y}^{\u2032}+6y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={5}^{2}-4(1)(6) & =1>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{\u2033}+2{y}^{\u2032}+y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={2}^{2}-4(1)(1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{\u2033}+4{y}^{\u2032}+2y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +2=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =8>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement III is correct.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\".",
